@page "/definitions"
@page "/specs"
@using RioCharts.Core.Definitions

<PageTitle>Chart definitions · RioCharts for Blazor and .NET</PageTitle>

<h1>Chart definitions</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A chart definition describes a chart as a small JSON object: which data to read, which fields go where, and how it should look. AI agents
    write it, apps save it, and the same definition draws with <code>&lt;RioChart Definition="..." /&gt;</code>, on the server, or through
    the <a href="ai">create_chart tool</a>.
</p>
<p>
    This page explains the format, one idea at a time, with each definition next to the chart it draws. At the end you can
    <a href="definitions#try-it">try it</a> yourself. To let people build charts without writing JSON, see the
    <a href="dashboards">chart editor</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="first">A first definition</h2>
<p>
    A definition names a <strong>dataset</strong> and, for each series, the <strong>fields</strong> (columns) that go along the x axis and
    up the y axis. Everything else has a default. This one sums the revenue per month and draws it as a line.
</p>
<DefinitionExample Title="Revenue per month" Json="@Minimal" />

<h2 id="data">Data: datasets and fields</h2>
<p>
    The definition holds no data itself. Your app passes in named datasets, tables with one value per row for each field, and the definition
    refers to them by name. Field types (time, number, text) are read from the values. Because only names travel, a definition is small,
    safe to save, and never contains anyone's data. These are the datasets the examples on this page read:
</p>
<DatasetPreview Datasets="PageDatasets" Used="shop" />
<p>
    A small definition can also carry its own rows in <code>"data"</code> (at most @ChartDefinitionJson.MaxInlineRows), for a few values an
    agent already has. How your app builds datasets is under <a href="definitions#datasets">where the data comes from</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="types">Chart types</h2>
<p>
    <code>type</code> picks the chart. Most read <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>; some read more fields, such as a range bar's
    <code>start</code> and <code>end</code>. With <code>auto</code>, the chart picks a type from the data: time along x gives a line,
    categories give bars, and shares that add up to 100 give a donut. Common names from other libraries are accepted too:
    <code>column</code>, <code>doughnut</code>, <code>spline</code>, <code>stackedBar</code>.
</p>
<DefinitionExample Title="Shares from inline rows" Json="@Shares">
    <Description><p>Inline rows and <code>auto</code>: four shares that add up to 100 become a donut.</p></Description>
</DefinitionExample>
<table class="docs">
    <tr><th>Type</th><th>What it reads</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>line</code>, <code>area</code>, <code>bar</code></td><td><code>x</code> (time, number or category) and <code>y</code>. A <code>stack</code> name stacks series; <code>"horizontal": true</code> on the chart lays bars sideways.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>pie</code>, <code>funnel</code>, <code>radialBar</code></td><td><code>x</code> names the slice or stage, <code>y</code> its size. <code>innerRadius</code> makes a donut.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>scatter</code></td><td>Two numbers, <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>; a <code>size</code> field makes bubbles.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>heatmap</code>, <code>calendar</code></td><td>A heatmap: <code>x</code> columns, <code>groupBy</code> rows, <code>y</code> the color. A calendar: a time <code>x</code>, one square per day.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>histogram</code>, <code>boxPlot</code></td><td>How values spread: a histogram counts a number field in bins; a box plot shows each group's quartiles.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>rangeBar</code>, <code>dumbbell</code>, <code>rangeArea</code></td><td>From–to values: a timeline's <code>row</code>, <code>start</code> and <code>end</code>; a dumbbell's <code>from</code> and <code>y</code>; a band's <code>low</code> and <code>y</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>waterfall</code>, <code>candlestick</code></td><td>Steps that add up (with <code>total</code> rows), and prices with <code>open</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>low</code> and <code>y</code> as the close.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gauge</code>, <code>bullet</code></td><td>One value against a scale (<code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>target</code>), or a value per row against its <code>targetField</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>treemap</code>, <code>radar</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>mapBubble</code></td><td>Nested groups (<code>path</code>), spokes, countries, and places by <code>latitude</code> and <code>longitude</code>.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="shaping">Shaping the data</h2>
<p>
    A series can reshape its rows before it draws, so the data can stay as it is stored:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>filter</code> keeps the rows that match all conditions: <code>[{ "field": "channel", "op": "eq", "value": "App" }]</code>, with eq, ne, gt, gte, lt, lte and contains.</li>
    <li><code>timeBucket</code> groups a time x per minute, hour, day, week, month, quarter or year (or <code>auto</code>), and <code>aggregate</code> combines the rows in each group or category: sum, average, min, max, count or last.</li>
    <li><code>groupBy</code> makes one series per value of a field: one line per sales channel.</li>
    <li><code>sort</code> ranks the categories by value, and <code>limit</code> keeps the first N: a top 10.</li>
</ul>
<DefinitionExample Title="Revenue per day and channel" Json="@PerDay">
    <Description><p>Hourly rows grouped per day, summed, one series per channel, stacked.</p></Description>
</DefinitionExample>
<DefinitionExample Title="Top three categories" Json="@TopThree">
    <Description><p>Monthly rows summed per category, sorted, the top three kept, laid sideways.</p></Description>
</DefinitionExample>

<h2 id="layout">Axes, legend and annotations</h2>
<p>
    The rest is the same model as the Blazor components: axis titles, formats and ranges (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code> and more vertical
    axes in <code>yAxes</code>), the <code>legend</code>, the <code>tooltip</code>, reference lines and bands in <code>annotations</code>, and the
    chart's <code>timeZone</code>. A series picks its axis with <code>yAxisId</code>.
</p>
<DefinitionExample Title="Two axes and a goal line" Json="@TwoAxes" />

<h2 id="errors">Errors that say what to do</h2>
<p>
    A definition is checked when it is read and again when it meets the data. Each error names the path to the problem and what is valid, so
    a person or an agent can fix it and try again. Small slips are read anyway, with a warning: <code>"timeBucket": "daily"</code> means day,
    <code>"type": "column"</code> means bar. Here the field names are wrong:
</p>
<DefinitionExample Title="A definition with errors" Json="@WithErrors" Height="120" />

<h2 id="versions">Saved definitions keep working</h2>
<p>
    A definition can say which format it is in: <code>"version": 1</code>. It is optional (missing means 1), and
    <code>ChartDefinitionJson.Serialize</code> writes it. Every RioCharts 1.x reads version 1 definitions: fields, chart type names,
    their synonyms and option words may be added, but none is removed or changes meaning, so a definition you save today renders in
    every later 1.x. A definition from a newer version is still read, with a warning, and fields this version does not know are left out.
</p>

<h2 id="datasets">Where the data comes from</h2>
<p>
    Build datasets from your own rows, from CSV, or let an <code>IChartDataSource</code> load them when a definition asks
    (e.g. <code>FolderDataSource</code> for a folder of CSV and JSON files). An optional description tells an agent what the data means.
    <code>ChartDefinitionJson.Schema()</code> returns the JSON schema of a definition with a description of every option.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@DatasetsCode" />
<CodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@ChartCode" />

<section class="example" id="try-it">
    <h2><a href="definitions#try-it" class="anchor">Try it<span class="anchor-mark" aria-hidden="true">#</span></a></h2>
    <div class="example-text">
        <p>
            Edit the definition; it is read, checked and drawn in your browser. Start from an example, and use the datasets above.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="example-card playground">
        <div class="examples-buttons">
            @foreach (var (name, json) in TryExamples)
            {
                <button class="small" @onclick="() => Load(json)">@name</button>
            }
        </div>

        <div class="try-grid">
            @* A transparent textarea over a highlighted copy of its text. Both wrap the same way and the
               highlighted copy sets the height, so they stay aligned without scroll syncing. *@
            <div class="spec-editor">
                <pre aria-hidden="true"><code>@((MarkupString)_highlighted)</code></pre>
                <textarea spellcheck="false" aria-label="Chart definition (JSON)" @bind="_json" @bind:event="oninput" @bind:after="Render"></textarea>
            </div>
            <div class="try-output">
                <div class="example-live">
                    @if (_spec is not null)
                    {
                        <RioChart TItem="object" Definition="_spec" Datasets="DefinitionDatasets.ByName" Height="300" Zoomable="true" />
                    }
                </div>
                @if (_errors.Count > 0)
                {
                    <ul class="spec-messages errors">
                        @foreach (var error in _errors)
                        {
                            <li>@error</li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
                @if (_warnings.Count > 0)
                {
                    <ul class="spec-messages">
                        @foreach (var warning in _warnings)
                        {
                            <li>@warning</li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
        @if (_normalized is not null)
        {
            <details class="example-code">
                <summary>
                    <svg class="chevron" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 4l4 4-4 4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" /></svg>
                    The definition as read
                </summary>
                <CodeBlock Language="json" Code="@_normalized" />
            </details>
        }
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<ChartDataset> PageDatasets = [.. DefinitionDatasets.ByName.Values];

    private const string Minimal = """
        {
          "dataset": "sales",
          "series": [{ "type": "line", "x": "month", "y": "revenue", "aggregate": "sum" }]
        }
        """;

    private const string Shares = """
        {
          "data": [ { "source": "Search", "share": 48 }, { "source": "Social", "share": 27 },
                    { "source": "Email", "share": 15 }, { "source": "Direct", "share": 10 } ],
          "series": [{ "type": "auto", "x": "source", "y": "share" }]
        }
        """;

    private const string PerDay = """
        {
          "dataset": "shop",
          "y": { "title": "€" },
          "series": [{ "type": "bar", "x": "timestamp", "y": "revenue", "timeBucket": "day", "aggregate": "sum",
                       "groupBy": "channel", "stack": "channels", "format": "#,0 €" }]
        }
        """;

    private const string TopThree = """
        {
          "dataset": "sales",
          "horizontal": true,
          "series": [{ "type": "bar", "x": "category", "y": "revenue", "aggregate": "sum",
                       "sort": "descending", "limit": 3, "format": "0 k€" }]
        }
        """;

    private const string TwoAxes = """
        {
          "dataset": "shop",
          "y": { "title": "Sessions" },
          "yAxes": [{ "id": "eur", "title": "€" }],
          "series": [
            { "type": "bar", "x": "timestamp", "y": "revenue", "timeBucket": "day", "aggregate": "sum", "yAxisId": "eur", "name": "Revenue" },
            { "type": "line", "x": "timestamp", "y": "sessions", "timeBucket": "day", "aggregate": "sum", "name": "Sessions" }
          ],
          "annotations": [{ "value": 7000, "label": "Goal: 7,000 sessions" }]
        }
        """;

    private const string WithErrors = """
        {
          "dataset": "shop",
          "series": [{ "type": "line", "x": "time", "y": "visits" }]
        }
        """;

    private static readonly (string Name, string Json)[] TryExamples =
    [
        ("Minimal", Minimal),
        ("One line per channel", """
            {
              "dataset": "shop",
              "title": "Orders per channel",
              "y": { "title": "Orders per hour" },
              "series": [{ "type": "spline", "x": "timestamp", "y": "orders", "groupBy": "channel" }],
              "annotations": [{ "value": 20, "label": "Goal: 20 an hour" }]
            }
            """),
        ("Revenue per day", PerDay),
        ("Top three", TopThree),
        ("Shares (auto donut)", Shares),
        ("Two axes", TwoAxes),
        ("With errors", WithErrors),
    ];

    private const string DatasetsCode = """
        // One dictionary per row: field name -> value.
        var rows = traffic.Select(t => new Dictionary<string, object?>
        {
            ["timestamp"] = t.Hour,
            ["channel"] = t.Channel,
            ["orders"] = t.Orders,
        });

        var datasets = new Dictionary<string, ChartDataset>
        {
            ["shop"] = ChartDataset.FromRows("shop", rows,
                "Online store traffic per sales channel, hourly"),
            ["sales"] = ChartDataset.FromCsv("sales", File.ReadAllText("sales.csv"),
                "Revenue per category and month, k€"),
        };
        """;

    private const string ChartCode = """
        <RioChart Definition="definition" Datasets="datasets" />
        """;

    private string _json = "";
    private string _highlighted = "";
    private ChartDefinition? _spec;
    private string? _normalized;
    private List<string> _errors = [];
    private List<string> _warnings = [];

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Load(TryExamples[1].Json);

    private void Load(string json)
    {
        _json = json;
        Render();
    }

    private void Render()
    {
        // The trailing space keeps a final empty line as tall as the textarea's.
        _highlighted = CodeHighlighter.ToHtml(_json, "json") + " ";
        var parsed = ChartDefinitionJson.Parse(_json);
        _errors = parsed.Errors.Select(e => e.ToString()).ToList();
        _warnings = [.. parsed.Warnings];
        _spec = parsed.Definition;
        _normalized = _spec is null ? null : ChartDefinitionJson.Serialize(_spec);
        if (_spec is not null)
        {
            var bound = ChartDefinitionBinder.Bind(_spec, DefinitionDatasets.ByName);
            _errors.AddRange(bound.Errors.Select(e => e.ToString()));
            _warnings.AddRange(bound.Warnings);
        }
    }
}
